@game "C:/Program Files (x86)/Steam/steamapps/common/The Last of Us Part II/"
@target "ss-t2/ss-workbench.bin"
@mod "hub_skip_upgrade_anim"

using #wait-for as far (symbol) -> u0;
using #interactable-get-def-id as far (symbol) -> symbol;
using #set-animation-phase as far (symbol, f32, u16, u16) -> symbol;

// ss-workbench drives the gun-upgrade "assembling" sequence from its apply-upgrade
// state. The actual upgrade work is spread across ~8 parallel tracks (apply-upgrade,
// mag, upgrade-mesh, bullets, weapon-strap, ui, attach, ...) and every one of them is
// gated on wait-until-anim-phase(player, <assemble-anim>, ...) -- i.e. the upgrade is
// choreographed to the player assemble animation's timeline. You therefore cannot just
// delete the animation; the apply tracks would hang on their phase waits.
//
// Instead we replace apply-upgrade@start@apply-upgrade@0 (vanilla: just
// `wait-for(curr-state-updated)`). curr-state-updated is signalled at the top of the
// animation track right after the assemble animation is started, so by the time this
// lambda runs the player is already playing the assemble anim. For the No Return hub
// theater workbench ONLY (interact-workbench-rogue-hub-theater), we snap that animation
// straight to its end frame: every phase-gated apply track immediately sees phase >= its
// target and fires the same frame, so the upgrade applies fully but instantly with no
// visible assembling animation. Every other workbench (main game + in-encounter rogue
// benches) takes the false branch and keeps the vanilla animation untouched.
u0 #apply-upgrade@start@apply-upgrade@0() {
    >> "here";
    #wait-for(#curr-state-updated);
    if (#interactable-get-def-id(#self) == #interact-workbench-rogue-hub-theater) {
        #set-animation-phase(#player, 1.0, 1, 0)!exe;
    }
}
